WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.981
The nightly settlement report sums each merchant's captured payments.

2
00:00:06.981 --> 00:00:11.759
In Spark it's a chain of steps on a DataFrame, a table split into partitions.

3
00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:16.651
Keep captured payments, convert cents to euros, group by merchant, sum.

4
00:00:16.651 --> 00:00:20.940
Each line returns at once, because Spark only writes down a plan.

5
00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:23.570
An action, like collect, makes it run.

6
00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:28.187
The driver, the process running your program, turns the plan into tasks.

7
00:00:28.367 --> 00:00:30.568
Four files make four partitions.

8
00:00:30.568 --> 00:00:36.209
Filtering and converting are narrow: each output row needs only its own partition.

9
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:45.921
So Spark chains them in one task per partition: read a file, filter, convert, add to a sum per merchant, with nothing written in between.

10
00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:47.822
Grouping is different.

11
00:00:47.822 --> 00:00:51.735
Merchant 42's total needs rows from all four files.

12
00:00:51.735 --> 00:00:53.193
That's a wide step.

13
00:00:53.367 --> 00:01:01.737
So each task writes its sums to disk in four parts, by merchant, and each task on the other side fetches one part from all four.

14
00:01:01.737 --> 00:01:02.980
That's the shuffle.

15
00:01:03.167 --> 00:01:09.317
No total can finish until all of that is written, so the shuffle cuts the job into two stages.

16
00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:18.868
Each stage runs one task per partition: four for the files, and four for the shuffle, set to four here instead of the default 200.

17
00:01:18.868 --> 00:01:21.237
One job, two stages, eight tasks.

18
00:01:21.433 --> 00:01:22.527
Stage 1 starts.

19
00:01:22.527 --> 00:01:27.633
Halfway through, task 2's machine dies, and its partial sums are lost.

20
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:29.186
Spark kept no copy.

21
00:01:29.186 --> 00:01:37.285
It knows how that partition was made, read file 2, filter, convert, sum, and runs exactly that again elsewhere.

22
00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:39.162
No other task is touched.

23
00:01:39.162 --> 00:01:43.368
That's safe because a task's output depends only on its input.

24
00:01:43.567 --> 00:01:48.779
Then stage 2 fetches its parts, adds them up, and the driver gets the totals.

25
00:01:48.933 --> 00:01:56.031
Spark plans lazily, chains narrow steps in one task per partition, and cuts a stage at every shuffle.

26
00:01:56.031 --> 00:01:59.123
A lost task just runs again from its inputs.
